  "result": {
    "summary": "Reviewed the full diff against the spec. claude_split matches the contract: clamped base, the +1 and -1 rules, and a pure function that tolerates an empty or partial card. Pool.claude_split() caches the split per instance and falls back to an empty card on any exception. eligible_executors applies the split only when both Claude rows exist and are enabled, so codex, haiku and a lone Claude row keep their row bands. status() now reads on_exhausted with a 'hold' default. pool.toml gets only the new [routing] table. The test headroom assertion was updated for the split and both acceptance tests are present. I did not run the test suite and I did not see the rest of the headroom test, so the comments below are low-severity nits.",
    "verdict": "approve",
    "comments": [
      {
        "path": "tests/test_pool.py",
        "line": 537,
        "issue": "The headroom test now expects claude:opus to be ineligible at complexity 3, so it no longer shows opus gated by account headroom in that step. Confirm another step in the test (or a new one) covers opus eligibility at a complexity above the split, such as 8.",
        "severity": "low"
      },
      {
        "path": "orchestrator/pool.py",
        "line": 216,
        "issue": "Pool.claude_split() calls scorecard.build() against live STATE, a disk read of all run logs per Pool instance. If Pool is built per call or per daemon tick, this adds I/O. Acceptable under the spec but worth watching.",
        "severity": "low"
      }
    ],
